// Phase 5A Task 4, design §11.6: a two-keyframe linear translation. 2 frames
// at 30fps (frameCount = 2, indices 0 and 1): `mover` starts at x=10 (frame 0)
// and reaches x=110 exactly at frame 1, so the Lottie doc carries exactly two
// position keyframes, t=0 and t=1. lottieEncode.test.ts already pins that the
// handles are present and explicit (§11.6 is settled from primary source —
// lottie-web 5.13.0's own PropertyFactory.js/BezierEaser.js).
//
// Sampling only frame 0.5 (the exact midpoint, x=60) does NOT discriminate
// linear from a symmetric ease — the review that caught this demonstrated a
// symmetric (0.42,0)/(0.58,1) ease also reads x=60 at frame 0.5, since any
// cubic bezier symmetric about (0.5,0.5) passes through the midpoint. Frame
// 0.25 discriminates: linear reads x=35 there; the symmetric counterfactual
// reads nothing (the shape has barely started moving, still under the sample
// window). Sample BOTH 0.25 and 0.5 — 0.5 alone answers nothing about the
// shape of the curve, only that some curve exists.
scene {
  size: (120, 40)
  duration: 0.0666666667
  background: #000000

  rectangle mover {
    position: (10, 20)
    size: (10, 10)
    color: #ffffff
    animate {
      property: position
      to: (110, 20)
      duration: 0.0333333333
    }
  }
}
